       nsswitch.conf - Name Service Switch configuration file

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  Name  Service Switch (NSS) configuration file, _/_e_t_c_/_n_s_s_w_i_t_c_h_._c_o_n_f,
       is used by the GNU C Library and certain other applications  to  deter‐
       mine  the  sources  from  which to obtain name‐service information in a
       range of categories, and in what order.  Each category  of  information
       is identified by a database name.

       The  file  is plain ASCII text, with columns separated by spaces or tab
       characters.  The first column specifies the database name.  The remain‐
       ing columns describe the order of sources to query and a limited set of
       actions that can be performed by lookup result.

       The following databases are understood by the GNU C Library:

       aalliiaasseess     Mail aliases, used by ggeettaalliiaasseenntt(3) and related functions.

       eetthheerrss      Ethernet numbers.

       ggrroouupp       Groups of users, used by ggeettggrreenntt(3) and related functions.

       hhoossttss       Host names and numbers, used by  ggeetthhoossttbbyynnaammee(3)  and  re‐
                   lated functions.

       iinniittggrroouuppss  Supplementary  group  access  list, used by ggeettggrroouupplliisstt(3)
                   function.

       nneettggrroouupp    Network‐wide list of  hosts  and  users,  used  for  access
                   rules.   C  libraries  before glibc 2.1 supported netgroups
                   only over NIS.

       nneettwwoorrkkss    Network names and numbers, used by ggeettnneetteenntt(3) and related
                   functions.

       ppaasssswwdd      User passwords, used by ggeettppwweenntt(3) and related functions.

       pprroottooccoollss   Network protocols, used by ggeettpprroottooeenntt(3) and related func‐
                   tions.

       ppuubblliicckkeeyy   Public and secret keys for Secure_RPC used by NFS and NIS+.

       rrppcc         Remote procedure call names and numbers, used by  ggeettrrppccbbyy‐‐
                   nnaammee(3) and related functions.

       sseerrvviicceess    Network  services,  used by ggeettsseerrvveenntt(3) and related func‐
                   tions.

       sshhaaddooww      Shadow user passwords,  used  by  ggeettssppnnaamm(3)  and  related
                   functions.

       The  GNU C Library ignores databases with unknown names.  Some applica‐
       tions use this to implement special handling for their  own  databases.
       For example, ssuuddoo(8) consults the ssuuddooeerrss database.  Delegation of sub‐
       ordinate  user/group  IDs  can  be configured using the ssuubbiidd database.
       Refer to ssuubbuuiidd(5) and ssuubbggiidd(5) for more details.

       Here is an example _/_e_t_c_/_n_s_s_w_i_t_c_h_._c_o_n_f file:

           passwd:         compat
           group:          compat
           shadow:         compat

           hosts:          dns [!UNAVAIL=return] files
           networks:       nis [NOTFOUND=return] files
           ethers:         nis [NOTFOUND=return] files
           protocols:      nis [NOTFOUND=return] files
           rpc:            nis [NOTFOUND=return] files
           services:       nis [NOTFOUND=return] files

       The first column is the database name.  The remaining columns specify:

       •  One or more service specifications, for example, "files",  "db",  or
          "nis".   The  order of the services on the line determines the order
          in which those services will be queried, in turn, until a result  is
          found.

       •  Optional  actions to perform if a particular result is obtained from
          the preceding service, for example, "[NOTFOUND=return]".

       The service specifications supported on your system depend on the pres‐
       ence of shared libraries,  and  are  therefore  extensible.   Libraries
       called  _/_l_i_b_/_l_i_b_n_s_s___S_E_R_V_I_C_E_._s_o_.XX  will provide the named _S_E_R_V_I_C_E.  On a
       standard installation, you can use "files", "db", "nis", and "nisplus".
       For the hhoossttss database, you can additionally specify  "dns".   For  the
       ppaasssswwdd, ggrroouupp, and sshhaaddooww databases, you can additionally specify "com‐
       pat" (see CCoommppaattiibbiilliittyy mmooddee below).  The version number XX may be 1 for
       glibc  2.0,  or  2 for glibc 2.1 and later.  On systems with additional
       libraries installed, you may have access to further  services  such  as
       "hesiod", "ldap", "winbind", and "wins".

       An action may also be specified following a service specification.  The
       action  modifies the behavior following a result obtained from the pre‐
       ceding data source.  Action items take the general form:

           [_S_T_A_T_U_S=_A_C_T_I_O_N]
           [!_S_T_A_T_U_S=_A_C_T_I_O_N]

       where

           _S_T_A_T_U_S => ssuucccceessss | nnoottffoouunndd | uunnaavvaaiill | ttrryyaaggaaiinn
           _A_C_T_I_O_N => rreettuurrnn | ccoonnttiinnuuee | mmeerrggee

       The ! negates the test, matching all possible results  except  the  one
       specified.  The case of the keywords is not significant.

       The  _S_T_A_T_U_S  value is matched against the result of the lookup function
       called by the preceding service specification, and can be one of:

           ssuucccceessss     No error occurred and the requested entry is  returned.
                       The default action for this condition is "return".

           nnoottffoouunndd    The  lookup  succeeded, but the requested entry was not
                       found.  The default action for this condition is  "con‐
                       tinue".

           uunnaavvaaiill     The  service is permanently unavailable.  This can mean
                       either that the required file cannot be read,  or,  for
                       network  services,  that the server is not available or
                       does not allow queries.  The default  action  for  this
                       condition is "continue".

           ttrryyaaggaaiinn    The  service  is  temporarily  unavailable.  This could
                       mean a file is locked or a server currently cannot  ac‐
                       cept  more  connections.   The  default action for this
                       condition is "continue".

       The _A_C_T_I_O_N value can be one of:

           rreettuurrnn      Return a result now.  Do not call  any  further  lookup
                       functions.  However, for compatibility reasons, if this
                       is  the  selected action for the ggrroouupp database and the
                       nnoottffoouunndd status, and the configuration  file  does  not
                       contain  the  iinniittggrroouuppss line, the next lookup function
                       is always called, without affecting the search result.

           ccoonnttiinnuuee    Call the next lookup function.

           mmeerrggee       _[_S_U_C_C_E_S_S_=_m_e_r_g_e_] is used between two  database  entries.
                       When  a  group is located in the first of the two group
                       entries, processing will continue on to the  next  one.
                       If  the  group is also found in the next entry (and the
                       group name and GID are an exact match), the member list
                       of the second entry will be added to the  group  object
                       to be returned.  Available since glibc 2.24.  Note that
                       merging  will  not be done for ggeettggrreenntt(3) nor will du‐
                       plicate members be pruned when they occur in  both  en‐
                       tries being merged.

